le tag settings, record image digests, and protect production repositories from unauthorized writes. Apply lifecycle policies only after validating which images are safe to expire. Review vulnerability findings and update vulnerable base images and packages. Configure encryption and repository access deliberately. For regional or cross-account distribution, verify destination availability and digest consistency before changing workload image references.\n\n## 9. Validation Checklist\n\n- Confirm the AWS account, region, and caller identity.\n- Confirm the ECR registry endpoint and repository URI.\n- Verify the required IAM and repository permissions.\n- Confirm Docker authentication uses a current ECR token.\n- Verify the image tag and digest in the registry.\n- Check encryption, scanning, and retention settings where applicable.\n- Validate that the intended consumer can retrieve the image.\n- Record the artifact digest and pipeline execution details.\n\n## 10. Learning Outcome\n\nAfter completing 'ECR Registry Architecture — Registry Fundamentals and Architecture — ECR Registry Architecture [stage-9-topic-2-section-15]', an engineer should be able to explain the concept within 'ECR Registry Architecture', identify the AWS components and permissions involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting registry state, troubleshoot common failures, and apply the capability safely in an automated production delivery workflow.\n\n## Section Reference\n\nNavigation Topic: ECR Registry Architecture\nSection Title: ECR Registry Architecture — Registry Fundamentals and Architecture — ECR Registry Architecture [stage-9-topic-2-section-15]\nSection ID: stage-9-topic-2-section-15\nService: Amazon Elastic Container Registry"
