        "content": "# Immutable Image Tags — Troubleshooting and Recovery\n\n## 1. Navigation Context\n\nThis section belongs to 'Immutable Image Tags' in the Amazon ECR learning path. It explains the specific engineering concept represented by the section title and its relationship with AWS container image storage and distribution.\n\n## 2. Amazon ECR Technical Explanation\n\nECR tag immutability prevents an existing image tag from being overwritten by another image. This protects release references from accidental replacement and helps maintain consistent artifact promotion. A pipeline must use a new tag for each distinct build when immutability is enabled. Teams should choose a tag strategy based on commit IDs, release versions, or build identifiers. Immutability does not replace digest verification, IAM restrictions, or image retention policies.\n\n## 3. Section-Level Engineering Details\n\nExplain the difference between a mutable image tag and an immutable content digest. Describe how manifests identify image configuration and layers, how tags reference images, and how release pipelines should record artifact identity. Include the implications for reproducible deployments, rollback, multi-platform images, and preventing accidental replacement of approved artifacts.\n\nThe implementation must be evaluated using the actual AWS account, region, repository, principal, and image reference. ECR operations are regional, and a correct repository name in the wrong region can still produce a not-found or authorization error. Record the complete registry URI and caller identity when validating an operation.\n\n## 4. Practical AWS CLI and Docker Commands\n\naws ecr describe-repositories --repository-names devmavox-app --region ap-south-1\naws ecr put-image-tag-mutability --repository-name devmavox-app --image-tag-mutability IMMUTABLE --region ap-south-1\naws ecr describe-images --repository-name devmavox-app --region ap-south-1\n\nReplace ACCOUNT_ID, KEY_ID, repository names, image tags, and region values with the real environment values. Do not paste credentials into scripts or source control. Review command help and permissions before making changes to production repositories.\n\n## 5. CI/CD Pipeline Integration\n\nA practical pipeline checks out source code, runs tests, builds a container image, applies a traceable tag, authenticates to ECR using its execution role, and publishes the artifact. After publication, the pipeline should retrieve the image metadata and record the digest. A later deployment stage should consume the approved digest rather than independently rebuilding the image. This creates a traceable relationship between source commit, pipeline execution, security results, registry artifact, and deployed release.\n\n## 6. IAM and Security Considerations\n\nUse separate identities for publishing and consuming images. Grant only the ECR actions required by each workflow and scope repository operations to the intended resources where supported. Protect authorization tokens, use TLS, review repository policies, and validate KMS permissions when customer-managed encryption is configured. Cross-account access should identify the exact external role and repository actions rather than granting broad account access.\n\n## 7. Troubleshooting and Root Cause Analysis\n\nStart with aws sts get-caller-identity and confirm the account and region. Check whether the repository exists, whether the image tag or digest is present, and whether Docker authenticated to the correct registry endpoint. Review IAM identity policies, repository resource policies, explicit denies, KMS permissions, network connectivity, and relevant CloudTrail events. Distinguish authentication failures, authorization failures, missing resources, and interrupted layer transfers. Apply the smallest corrective change and repeat the original operation.\n\n## 8. Production Best Practices\n\nUse unique release tags or immutable tag settings, record image digests, and protect production repositories from unauthorized writes. Apply lifecycle policies only after validating which images are safe to expire. Review vulnerability findings and update vulnerable base images and packages. Configure encryption and repository access deliberately. For regional or cross-account distribution, verify destination availability and digest consistency before changing workload image references.\n\n## 9. Validation Checklist\n\n- Confirm the AWS account, region, and caller identity.\n- Confirm the ECR registry endpoint and repository URI.\n- Verify the required IAM and repository permissions.\n- Confirm Docker authentication uses a current ECR token.\n- Verify the image tag and digest in the registry.\n- Check encryption, scanning, and retention settings where applicable.\n- Validate that the intended consumer can retrieve the image.\n- Record the artifact digest and pipeline execution details.\n\n## 10. Learning Outcome\n\nAfter completing 'Immutable Image Tags — Troubleshooting and Recovery', an engineer should be able to explain the concept within 'Immutable Image Tags', identify the AWS components and permissions involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting registry state, troubleshoot common failures, and apply the capability safely in an automated production delivery workflow.\n\n## Section Reference\n\nNavigation Topic: Immutable Image Tags\nSection Title: Immutable Image Tags — Troubleshooting and Recovery\nSection ID: stage-9-topic-9-section-12\nService: Amazon Elastic Container Registry"
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-9-topic-9-section-13",
