        "content": "# Encryption and Access Control — Image Immutability\n\n## 1. Navigation Context\n\nThis section belongs to 'Encryption and Access Control' in the Amazon ECR learning path. It explains the specific engineering concept represented by the section title and its relationship with AWS container image storage and distribution.\n\n## 2. Amazon ECR Technical Explanation\n\nECR protects image data at rest using encryption and supports AWS-managed or customer-managed AWS KMS keys according to repository configuration and service capabilities. Access is controlled through IAM identity policies, repository resource policies, and organizational security controls. Teams should restrict repository write operations, review cross-account grants, protect KMS key permissions, and enforce TLS for registry communication. Encryption does not replace authorization: a principal must still be permitted to access the repository.\n\n## 3. Section-Level Engineering Details\n\nExplain the security control being applied, the resources it protects, and the configuration needed to enforce it. Cover the relevant AWS service integration, permissions, evidence, limitations, and operational ownership. Describe how teams should review findings or access decisions, prioritize remediation, and verify the control after changes.\n\nThe implementation must be evaluated using the actual AWS account, region, repository, principal, and image reference. ECR operations are regional, and a correct repository name in the wrong region can still produce a not-found or authorization error. Record the complete registry URI and caller identity when validating an operation.\n\n## 4. Practical AWS CLI and Docker Commands\n\naws ecr describe-repositories --repository-names devmavox-app --region ap-south-1\naws ecr get-repository-policy --repository-name devmavox-app --region ap-south-1\naws kms describe-key --key-id KEY_ID --region ap-south-1\n\nReplace ACCOUNT_ID, KEY_ID, repository names, image tags,